| 11.5  | 35.5  | 31.2  | 21.8\nL196: 2004  | 100.0  | 11.2  | 36.2  | 30.3  | 22.3\nL197: 2005  | 100.0  | 11.2  | 36.4  | 30.2  | 22.2\nL198: 2006  | 100.0  | 11.0  | 35.2  | 30.6  | 23.2\nL199: 2007  | 100.0  | 10.0  | 35.0  | 30.9  | 24.0\nL200: 2008  | 100.0  | 9.6  | 34.7  | 31.8  | 23.8\nL201: 2009  | 100.0  | 9.3  | 34.0  | 32.4  | 24.3\nL202: 2010  | 100.0  | 9.4  | 33.3  | 32.8  | 24.5\nL203: 2011  | 100.0  | 8.9  | 33.6  | 32.8  | 24.7\nL204: 2012  | 100.0  | 8.3  | 32.8  | 32.7  | 26.2\nL205: 2013  | 100.0  | 8.6  | 31.8  | 32.8  | 26.9\nL206: 2014  | 100.0  | 8.2  | 31.3  | 33.1  | 27.3\nL207: 2015  | 100.0  | 7.5  | 30.9  | 33.3  | 28.3\nL208: 2016  | 100.0  | 7.5  | 31.1  | 32.6  | 28.8\nL209: 2017  | 100.0  | 6.9  | 31.0  | 32.5  | 29.6\nL210: 2018  | 100.0  | 6.4  | 30.6  | 31.8  | 31.2\nL211: In 2018, 31 percent of Blacks in the labor force had earned a bachelor’s degree and higher, compared with 16 percent in 1992. Another 32 percent of Blacks in the labor force had completed some college or an associate degree in 2018. Just 6 percent of Blacks age 25 and older in the labor force had less than a high school diploma in 2018, down from 18 percent in 1992.\nL212: In 2018, the unemployment rate for Blacks age 25 and older with less than a high school diploma was 10.4 percent. That was about three and a half times the rate for Blacks with a bachelor’s degree and higher (2.9 percent).\nL213: \nL214:   * 【151†Chart Image】\nL215:   * 【152†Chart Data】\nL216: Unemployment rates of Blacks or African Americans age 25 and older by educational attainment, 1992–2018 annual averages\nL217: Year  | Total  | Less than a\nL218: high school diploma  | High school graduates,\nL219: no college  | Some college or\nL220: associate degree  | Bachelor's degree\nL221: and higher\nL222: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL223: 1992  | 10.9%  | 15.3%  | 12.3%  | 9.8%  | 4.4%\nL224: 1993  | 9.8  | 15.4  | 10.8  | 8.7  | 3.8\nL225: 1994  | 8.6  | 13.5  | 9.9  | 7.5  | 3.5\nL226: 1995  | 7.4  | 12.4  | 8.2  | 6.7  | 3.2\nL227: 1996  | 7.7  | 12.6  | 9.1  | 6.4  | 3.1\nL228: 1997  | 7.3  | 13.1  | 8.1  | 5.6  | 3.6\nL229: 1998  | 6.4  | 11.6  | 7.4  | 5.1  | 2.9\nL230: 1999  | 5.7  | 11.6  | 6.3  | 4.4  | 2.7\nL231: 2000  | 5.4  | 10.7  | 6.4  | 4.0  | 2.5\nL232: 2001  | 6.2  | 11.8  | 7.4  | 5.0  | 2.7\nL233: 2002  | 7.7  | 13.3  | 8.8  | 6.7  | 4.2\nL234: 2003  | 8.3  | 13.9  | 9.3  | 7.9  | 4.5\nL235: 2004  | 8.1  | 15.5  | 8.7  | 7.6  | 4.3\nL236: 2005  | 7.5  | 14.4  | 8.5  | 6.9  | 3.5\nL237: 2006  | 6.8  | 12.8  | 8.0  | 6.2  | 2.8\nL238: 2007  | 6.2  | 12.0  | 7.3  | 5.5  | 3.0\nL239: 2008  | 7.9  | 14.5  | 9.3  | 7.4  | 4.0\nL240: 2009  | 12.3  | 21.3  | 14.0  | 11.5  | 7.3\nL241: 2010  | 13.4  | 22.5  | 15.8  | 12.4  | 7.9\nL242: 2011  | 13.4  | 24.6  | 15.5  | 13.1  | 7.1\nL243: 2012  | 11.4  | 20.4  | 13.4  | 11.1  | 6.3\nL244: 2013  | 10.5  | 20.5  | 12.6  | 9.8  | 5.7\nL245: 2014  | 9.1  | 17.2  | 10.7  | 8.9  | 5.2\nL246: 2015  | 7.8  | 15.9  | 9.7  | 7.4  | 4.0\nL247: 2016  | 6.8  | 14.1  | 8.6  | 6.1  | 3.7\nL248: 2017  | 6.2  | 12.5  | 7.8  | 5.8  | 3.6\nL249: 2018  | 5.3  | 10.4  | 6.7  | 5.2  | 2.9\nL250: The unemployment rate for Black high school graduates (no college) was 6.7 percent in 2018. The rate for Blacks with some college or an associate degree was 5.2 percent.\nL251: \nL252: These data are from the 【52†Current Population Survey】. For more data about race and ethnicity, see 【153†our demographics page】.\nL253: \nL254: RELATED SUBJECTS\nL255: \nL256:   * 【154†Education and Training】\nL257:   * 【155†Labor Force】\nL258: \nL259:   * 【109†Publications】\nL260:   * 【110†The Economics Daily】\nL261: \nL262: SUGGESTED CITATION\nL263: Bureau of Labor Statistics, U.S. Department of Labor, The Economics Daily, Rising educational attainment among Blacks or African Americans in the labor force, 1992 to 2018 at 【156†https://www.bls.gov/opub/ted/2019/rising-educational-attainment-among-blacks-or-african-americans-in-the-labor-f